{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Wt3xgYngnQV1mBN3VdVHVo","type":"tool_result","content":"39:The classical cover-time bounds $2m(n-1)$ and $(4/27)n^3$ hold for every graph but can be loose on concrete small graphs. We ask whether six cheap statistics, namely vertex and edge counts, mean, maximum and minimum degree, and diameter, predict the expected cover time of connected graphs with $5\\le n\\le 30$ more accurately, and how the error depends on graph type. An untuned log-linear ridge regression is fitted to Monte Carlo labels on 117 graphs from ten synthetic families. It is scored on 116 held-out graphs that are isomorphism-disjoint from the training graphs, with a paired two-level bootstrap. Its mean absolute log error is 0.183 (95\\% CI 0.167--0.207), a typical error factor of 1.20, against 1.397 (factor 4.0) for the better bound. The paired advantage is significant in 9 of 10 families and largest where the bounds are loosest (complete, Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi, random 3-regular). On lollipops, where the cubic bound is nearly tight, the advantage is not statistically clear. Fitted prediction helps most far from the worst case, but it carries no guarantee outside the studied families and sizes.\n46:Start a simple random walk at a vertex of a connected undirected graph $G=(V,E)$ and let it step to a uniformly random neighbour until every vertex has been visited. The expected number of steps is the \\emph{cover time}. It governs randomized graph exploration and the space-efficient connectivity algorithm of \\citet{Aleliunas1979RandomWU}, and more generally any procedure that must touch every state of a reversible Markov chain \\citep{Lovász2001RandomWO}. The theory is mature. \\citet{Aleliunas1979RandomWU} showed that the cover time of any connected graph with $n$ vertices and $m$ edges is at most $2m(n-1)$. Feige later showed that the worst case over all $n$-vertex graphs is $(4/27)n^3$ to leading order, attained asymptotically by lollipop graphs, and that every connected graph needs at least about $n\\ln n$ steps \\citep{Feige1995ATL}. These results are tight \\emph{over the class of all graphs}. For a specific small graph that is far from the extremal shape, both bounds can overshoot the true cover time by a large factor.\n48:Practitioners who need a number for a given small graph, for example to set a walk budget or sanity-check a simulator, therefore face a gap. The worst-case bounds are cheap but loose. Exact computation or long simulation is accurate but needs the whole graph and substantial compute. Tighter graph-aware bounds also need full structural information, such as all pairwise hitting times or effective resistances \\citep{Chandra1989TheER}. This motivates a narrow empirical question that the literature leaves open:\n52:We answer with a controlled, reproducible measurement rather than a new model. The predictor is a deliberately simple, untuned log-linear ridge regression on the logarithms of the six statistics plus one interaction term. It is fitted on Monte Carlo estimates of the uniform-start cover time. All accuracy claims come from a single sealed evaluation on held-out graphs that are isomorphism-disjoint from the training graphs, drawn from ten synthetic families: paths, cycles, stars, complete graphs, 2D grids, binary trees, lollipops, random trees, Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi graphs and random 3-regular graphs. Uncertainty is quantified by a two-level bootstrap that resamples both graphs and Monte Carlo walks. Comparisons with each bound use paired resamples.\n57:\\item \\textbf{A family-resolved reliability map.} We report per-family errors and paired gains with confidence intervals. The predictor is most accurate on stars, random 3-regular graphs, random trees and paths, and least accurate on complete, grid and Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi graphs. Its advantage over the bounds is significant in nine families and not statistically clear on lollipops (\\S\\ref{sec:family}).\n58:\\item \\textbf{A mechanistic reading of where fitted prediction helps.} The per-family gain tracks the slack of the best bound. Gains are smallest on the two families near the bounds' extremal shapes (lollipop and path) and largest on dense and expander-like families, whose cover times are close to the $n\\ln n$ lower-bound regime (\\S\\ref{sec:discussion}).\n70:which averages over a uniformly random starting vertex. The spanning-tree argument of \\citet{Aleliunas1979RandomWU} gives $\\Ccov(G)\\le 2m(n-1)$ for every start vertex. The bound overestimates dense graphs badly: for the complete graph $K_n$ it is $n(n-1)^2$, whereas $\\Ccov(K_n)=(n-1)H_{n-1}$, where $H_k$ is the $k$-th harmonic number \\citep{Lovász2001RandomWO}. Feige's upper bound $\\tfrac{4}{27}n^3+o(n^3)$ is the exact worst case over $n$-vertex graphs and is approached by lollipops, i.e.\\ a clique of about $2n/3$ vertices joined to a path. At the other extreme, every connected graph has cover time at least $(1-o(1))\\,n\\ln n$, which the complete graph approaches \\citep{Feige1995ATL}. The ratio between the two extremes grows like $n^2/\\ln n$. Even at $n\\le 30$, true cover times therefore span orders of magnitude across graph shapes. A bound that depends only on $n$ and $m$ cannot be accurate across that whole range.\n73:Tighter estimates use more structure. Matthews' method bounds the cover time by the maximum hitting time times $H_{n-1}$. \\citet{Chandra1989TheER} showed that commute times equal $2m$ times the effective resistance between the endpoints. As a result, the cover time lies within a logarithmic factor of $m\\,R_{\\max}$, where $R_{\\max}$ is the largest effective resistance between any two vertices. Since $R_{\\max}$ is at most the diameter, the product $m\\cdot\\diam(G)$ is a natural cheap proxy for this resistance scale. Exact hitting times and effective resistances need a linear solve on the full adjacency matrix, so they are not ``cheap statistics'' in our sense. We still include a Matthews-type reference (maximum hitting time times $H_{n-1}$) as a graph-aware comparison point. The survey of \\citet{Lovász2001RandomWO} collects the classical closed forms for paths, cycles, complete graphs and stars. These closed forms are used here only to check the simulator (\\S\\ref{sec:protocol}).\n76:Prior work on cover time is overwhelmingly analytic: it derives bounds that hold for all graphs, or asymptotics for specific families. We are not aware of a controlled, held-out measurement of how well a few global statistics predict the cover time of concrete small graphs, broken down by graph family, with Monte Carlo and graph-sampling uncertainty propagated into the comparison. Our study is complementary to the analytic results, not a substitute. Bounds come with guarantees, whereas a fitted predictor is only as good as the match between training and test distributions. We therefore treat the bounds \\emph{as predictors}, i.e.\\ we score them by how close they are to the true value, and we limit every claim to the evaluated families and sizes.\n103:The classical results suggest that, within a fixed structural regime, the cover time behaves roughly like a product of powers of size and geometry. Examples are $(n-1)H_{n-1}\\approx n\\ln n$ for complete graphs, $n(n-1)/2$ for cycles, $\\Theta(n^2)$ for paths, $\\Theta(n^3)$ for lollipops, and $m\\,R_{\\max}$ up to a logarithmic factor in general \\citep{Feige1995ATL,Chandra1989TheER,Lovász2001RandomWO}. A product of powers is linear in log space. This motivates a log-linear model in $\\ln n$, $\\ln m$ and $\\ln \\diam$. Degree statistics are added because they separate regimes with the same $(n,m)$. For example, a star and a path with the same $n$ have equal $m$ but very different $d_{\\max}$ and diameter, and their cover times differ by a factor that grows roughly like $n/\\ln n$. The minimum degree reflects bottlenecks such as pendant paths, which dominate lollipop cover times. An interaction term $\\ln n\\cdot\\ln\\diam$ lets the effective exponent on the diameter vary with size. This allows, for instance, a different diameter dependence for long thin graphs than for compact ones. We expected this representation to capture most of the variation \\emph{between} regimes, which is exactly what $n$- and $m$-only bounds miss. We also expected it to fit less well within families whose cover time is not a single power law in these statistics. These are design expectations; we do not isolate the contribution of individual terms (\\S\\ref{sec:limits}).\n117:\\item \\textbf{Generic bounds used as predictors:} $2m(n-1)$ \\citep{Aleliunas1979RandomWU}; the leading term $(4/27)n^3$ of Feige's upper bound; and their pointwise minimum, called the \\emph{best bound}. The best bound is the strongest predictor that uses only the generic bounds and is our primary comparator.\n118:\\item \\textbf{Matthews-type reference:} the exact maximum hitting time (from a linear solve on the full transition matrix) times $H_{n-1}$. It needs the whole graph, so it marks what full structural information gives a bound-based predictor.\n141:Random 3-regular & configuration model, simple, connected & 16 & 16 \\\\\n182:Matthews ref.\\ ($\\max H\\cdot H_{n-1}$) & full graph & 0.605 & 1.83 & --- \\\\\n190:% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.matthews_ref_log_err]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.best_bound_log_err]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.feige_lead_log_err]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.aklrr_log_err]]\n197:\\caption{Held-out mean absolute log error by predictor. (a) Fitted predictors and the permuted-feature control; the error bar on our predictor is its 95\\% two-level bootstrap CI. (b) Bound-based predictors. The Matthews reference needs the full graph. The x-axis is shared across panels.}\n206:The answer to the first research question is therefore yes, within the evaluated regime. Six statistics, combined by a model fitted on in-distribution labels, predict small-graph cover times far more accurately than the generic bounds evaluated at the same graphs. This should not be misread. The bounds hold for every connected graph, and the comparison scores them only on closeness to the true cover time, which they were never designed to optimize. The predictor offers no guarantee in either direction. Its errors are two-sided, and it can underestimate.\n208:A more demanding comparison is the Matthews-type reference, which uses exact hitting times from the full transition matrix. Its error is 0.605, better than either generic bound but still above that of the six-statistic predictor (point estimates; the evaluator reports no paired interval for this comparison). Global statistics plus fitted coefficients thus beat a structure-aware bound at this scale. The reason is that the fitted model is not constrained to overestimate: it learns the typical ratio between cover time and its scale, which a bound must cover with a worst-case constant.\n212:Three references bracket what the statistics contribute. A constant prediction (the training geometric mean) has error 0.942, which is already lower than both generic bounds. This underlines how far the bounds are from typical small-graph cover times. Fitting a size-only power law in $n$ and $m$ lowers the error to 0.526. Adding the degree and diameter statistics, in our predictor, lowers it further to 0.183. The paired interval for the difference between our predictor and the size-only reference is $[-0.391,-0.288]$. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.delta_vs_ols_ref_ci_high]]\n231: It is lowest on stars (0.056), random 3-regular graphs (0.059), random trees (0.062) and paths (0.092), all within about 10\\% of the true value in typical ratio terms. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.final_log_err_star]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.final_log_err_random_regular3]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.final_log_err_random_tree]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.final_log_err_path]]\n238:It is largest on random 3-regular graphs ($-2.088$, CI $[-2.303,-1.859]$), complete graphs ($-2.076$, CI $[-2.520,-1.608]$) and Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi graphs ($-2.072$, CI $[-2.494,-1.626]$). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.delta_vs_best_bound_random_regular3]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.delta_vs_best_bound_complete]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.delta_vs_best_bound_erdos_renyi]]\n252:A lollipop with a clique of about $2n/3$ vertices is the shape that asymptotically attains $(4/27)n^3$ \\citep{Feige1995ATL}. When a bound is nearly tight for a family, no predictor can beat it by much on that family. When a family lives near the opposite extreme, the bound must overshoot by a factor that grows with $n$, and any reasonable fitted model gains a lot. The latter case is the near-$n\\ln n$ regime of dense and expander-like graphs: complete graphs, dense Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi graphs and random 3-regular graphs. Our predictor does not need to understand the extremal combinatorics. It only needs to place a graph on the right scale, and the six statistics are enough for that.\n255:The following explanations are consistent with the measurements but were not tested by ablation. \\emph{Low error} on stars, paths, cycles and random 3-regular graphs may reflect families whose cover time is close to a single power law in $(n,m,\\diam,d_{\\max})$ and whose members have distinctive statistic vectors. For example, stars are identified by $d_{\\max}=n-1$ and $\\diam=2$, and paths by $\\diam=n-1$. \\emph{Higher error} on complete graphs may arise because all complete graphs have $\\diam=1$, which makes the interaction term vanish. The model must then express the $n\\ln n$ growth through $\\ln n$ and $\\ln m$ alone, sharing coefficients with sparse families of very different growth. For Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi graphs, the sampled edge density ranges from just above the connectivity threshold to $p=0.5$, so cover times move between sparse-tree-like and dense regimes, and the minimum degree only partly captures local bottlenecks. Grids contribute only six training graphs, with shapes from square to elongated. Their cover time may not follow a single power law in these statistics at this scale, which a log-linear surface cannot reproduce. Each of these hypotheses predicts that adding family-appropriate scaling terms (e.g.\\ $n H_n$, $m\\cdot\\diam/d_{\\min}$) would reduce the corresponding family error. That experiment is not part of the evidence reported here.\n262:For a practitioner with a small graph from one of the evaluated families, the six-statistic predictor gives a point estimate typically within about 20\\% of the expected cover time. It is more reliable on tree-like, regular and star-like graphs than on dense, random or grid graphs. When a guaranteed budget is required, the generic bounds remain the right tool, and their slack can be read off Figure~\\ref{fig:family}a: for lollipop-like graphs the cheaper of the two bounds is already accurate to within a typical factor of about 1.3.\n270:\\paragraph{No component attribution.} We evaluated one fixed configuration of the predictor. There are no ablations removing individual features, the interaction term or the ridge penalty, and no sweep over the regularization strength. The size-only reference differs in its whole feature set, so it shows that degree and diameter together add information, not which one matters. The explanations for per-family behaviour in \\S\\ref{sec:discussion} are hypotheses.\n274:\\paragraph{Per-family uncertainty and small families.} Per-family errors of the predictor are reported without intervals. Grids have only five held-out graphs and six training graphs. Per-family paired intervals exist for the gain over the best bound but not for the other comparators. The Matthews, constant and single-bound comparisons are point estimates without paired intervals, and we avoid significance language for them.\n281:We asked whether a handful of cheap global statistics can predict the cover time of small connected graphs more accurately than the classical general-purpose bounds, and how the answer depends on graph type. We evaluated an untuned log-linear ridge regression on the vertex count, edge count, three degree statistics and diameter. On held-out, isomorphism-disjoint graphs from ten families with $n\\le 30$, its typical error is a factor of about 1.20 (mean absolute log error 0.183, CI 0.167--0.207), against about 4.0 for the better of $2m(n-1)$ and $(4/27)n^3$. The paired advantage is significant in nine of ten families. It is largest on complete, Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi and random 3-regular graphs, where the bounds are loosest. On lollipops, where the cubic bound is nearly tight, there is no statistically clear advantage. The predictor itself is least reliable on complete, grid and Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi graphs, and twelve held-out graphs show that statistics-only prediction has an intrinsic ambiguity. Overall, worst-case bounds and fitted predictors answer different questions. The bounds guarantee; the predictor estimates, and it is most useful exactly where a graph is far from the worst case. Testing the stated hypotheses with targeted ablations, extending the size range, and adding richer cheap statistics are the natural next steps.\n312:Random 3-regular & 16 & 0.059 & 2.146 & $-2.088$ & $[-2.303,\\ -1.859]$ \\\\\n330:The evaluator (\\texttt{task/evaluate\\_final.py}) generates deterministic families from a public catalogue (\\texttt{det\\_catalogue}), with pool membership fixed by $n \\bmod 3$. Random families come from \\texttt{random\\_graph}: random trees from uniform Pr\\\"ufer sequences; Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi graphs with $n$ uniform on $\\{5,\\dots,30\\}$, $p$ uniform on $[2\\ln n/n, \\max(0.5, 2\\ln n/n)]$, rejected until connected; random 3-regular graphs via a configuration model with even $n\\in\\{6,\\dots,30\\}$, rejected until simple and connected. Labels come from \\texttt{simulate\\_cover\\_times} with 256 walks per graph. Bootstrap intervals use 1000 replicates of the two-level scheme in \\S\\ref{sec:protocol}. % @evidence[[analysis/roster.json#bootstrap_reps]]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b15765f-ba82-4d49-aaa3-a4d557e16332","uuid":"e38f3630-46d9-4a0f-b806-cb417beb0c68","timestamp":"2026-10-27T18:45:05.272Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"39:The classical cover-time bounds $2m(n-1)$ and $(4/27)n^3$ hold for every graph but can be loose on concrete small graphs. We ask whether six cheap statistics, namely vertex and edge counts, mean, maximum and minimum degree, and diameter, predict the expected cover time of connected graphs with $5\\le n\\le 30$ more accurately, and how the error depends on graph type. An untuned log-linear ridge regression is fitted to Monte Carlo labels on 117 graphs from ten synthetic families. It is scored on 116 held-out graphs that are isomorphism-disjoint from the training graphs, with a paired two-level bootstrap. Its mean absolute log error is 0.183 (95\\% CI 0.167--0.207), a typical error factor of 1.20, against 1.397 (factor 4.0) for the better bound. The paired advantage is significant in 9 of 10 families and largest where the bounds are loosest (complete, Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi, random 3-regular). On lollipops, where the cubic bound is nearly tight, the advantage is not statistically clear. Fitted prediction helps most far from the worst case, but it carries no guarantee outside the studied families and sizes.\n46:Start a simple random walk at a vertex of a connected undirected graph $G=(V,E)$ and let it step to a uniformly random neighbour until every vertex has been visited. The expected number of steps is the \\emph{cover time}. It governs randomized graph exploration and the space-efficient connectivity algorithm of \\cit