, $\\ln m$ and $\\ln \\diam$. Degree statistics are added because they separate regimes with the same $(n,m)$. For example, a star and a path with the same $n$ have equal $m$ but very different $d_{\\max}$ and diameter, and their cover times differ by a factor that grows roughly like $n/\\ln n$. The minimum degree reflects bottlenecks such as pendant paths, which dominate lollipop cover times. An interaction term $\\ln n\\cdot\\ln\\diam$ lets the effective exponent on the diameter vary with size. This allows, for instance, a different diameter dependence for long thin graphs than for compact ones. We expected this representation to capture most of the variation \\emph{between} regimes, which is exactly what $n$- and $m$-only bounds miss. We also expected it to fit less well within families whose cover time is not a single power law in these statistics. These are design expectations; we do not isolate the contribution of individual terms (\\S\\ref{sec:limits}).\n117:\\item \\textbf{Generic bounds used as predictors:} $2m(n-1)$ \\citep{Aleliunas1979RandomWU}; the leading term $(4/27)n^3$ of Feige's upper bound; and their pointwise minimum, called the \\emph{best bound}. The best bound is the strongest predictor that uses only the generic bounds and is our primary comparator.\n118:\\item \\textbf{Matthews-type reference:} the exact maximum hitting time (from a linear solve on the full transition matrix) times $H_{n-1}$. It needs the whole graph, so it marks what full structural information gives a bound-based predictor.\n141:Random 3-regular & configuration model, simple, connected & 16 & 16 \\\\\n182:Matthews ref.\\ ($\\max H\\cdot H_{n-1}$) & full graph & 0.605 & 1.83 & --- \\\\\n190:% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.matthews_ref_log_err]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.best_bound_log_err]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.feige_lead_log_err]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.aklrr_log_err]]\n197:\\caption{Held-out mean absolute log error by predictor. (a) Fitted predictors and the permuted-feature control; the error bar on our predictor is its 95\\% two-level bootstrap CI. (b) Bound-based predictors. The Matthews reference needs the full graph. The x-axis is shared across panels.}\n206:The answer to the first research question is therefore yes, within the evaluated regime. Six statistics, combined by a model fitted on in-distribution labels, predict small-graph cover times far more accurately than the generic bounds evaluated at the same graphs. This should not be misread. The bounds hold for every connected graph, and the comparison scores them only on closeness to the true cover time, which they were never designed to optimize. The predictor offers no guarantee in either direction. Its errors are two-sided, and it can underestimate.\n208:A more demanding comparison is the Matthews-type reference, which uses exact hitting times from the full transition matrix. Its error is 0.605, better than either generic bound but still above that of the six-statistic predictor (point estimates; the evaluator reports no paired interval for this comparison). Global statistics plus fitted coefficients thus beat a structure-aware bound at this scale. The reason is that the fitted model is not constrained to overestimate: it learns the typical ratio between cover time and its scale, which a bound must cover with a worst-case constant.\n212:Three references bracket what the statistics contribute. A constant prediction (the training geometric mean) has error 0.942, which is already lower than both generic bounds. This underlines how far the bounds are from typical small-graph cover times. Fitting a size-only power law in $n$ and $m$ lowers the error to 0.526. Adding the degree and diameter statistics, in our predictor, lowers it further to 0.183. The paired interval for the difference between our predictor and the size-only reference is $[-0.391,-0.288]$. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.delta_vs_ols_ref_ci_high]]\n231: It is lowest on stars (0.056), random 3-regular graphs (0.059), random trees (0.062) and paths (0.092), all within about 10\\% of the true value in typical ratio terms. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.final_log_err_star]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.final_log_err_random_regular3]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.final_log_err_random_tree]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.final_log_err_path]]\n238:It is largest on random 3-regular graphs ($-2.088$, CI $[-2.303,-1.859]$), complete graphs ($-2.076$, CI $[-2.520,-1.608]$) and Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi graphs ($-2.072$, CI $[-2.494,-1.626]$). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.delta_vs_best_bound_random_regular3]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.delta_vs_best_bound_complete]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.delta_vs_best_bound_erdos_renyi]]\n252:A lollipop with a clique of about $2n/3$ vertices is the shape that asymptotically attains $(4/27)n^3$ \\citep{Feige1995ATL}. When a bound is nearly tight for a family, no predictor can beat it by much on that family. When a family lives near the opposite extreme, the bound must overshoot by a factor that grows with $n$, and any reasonable fitted model gains a lot. The latter case is the near-$n\\ln n$ regime of dense and expander-like graphs: complete graphs, dense Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi graphs and random 3-regular graphs. Our predictor does not need to understand the extremal combinatorics. It only needs to place a graph on the right scale, and the six statistics are enough for that.\n255:The following explanations are consistent with the measurements but were not tested by ablation. \\emph{Low error} on stars, paths, cycles and random 3-regular graphs may reflect families whose cover time is close to a single power law in $(n,m,\\diam,d_{\\max})$ and whose members have distinctive statistic vectors. For example, stars are identified by $d_{\\max}=n-1$ and $\\diam=2$, and paths by $\\diam=n-1$. \\emph{Higher error} on complete graphs may arise because all complete graphs have $\\diam=1$, which makes the interaction term vanish. The model must then express the $n\\ln n$ growth through $\\ln n$ and $\\ln m$ alone, sharing coefficients with sparse families of very different growth. For Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi graphs, the sampled edge density ranges from just above the connectivity threshold to $p=0.5$, so cover times move between sparse-tree-like and dense regimes, and the minimum degree only partly captures local bottlenecks. Grids contribute only six training graphs, with shapes from square to elongated. Their cover time may not follow a single power law in these statistics at this scale, which a log-linear surface cannot reproduce. Each of these hypotheses predicts that adding family-appropriate scaling terms (e.g.\\ $n H_n$, $m\\cdot\\diam/d_{\\min}$) would reduce the corresponding family error. That experiment is not part of the evidence reported here.\n262:For a practitioner with a small graph from one of the evaluated families, the six-statistic predictor gives a point estimate typically within about 20\\% of the expected cover time. It is more reliable on tree-like, regular and star-like graphs than on dense, random or grid graphs. When a guaranteed budget is required, the generic bounds remain the right tool, and their slack can be read off Figure~\\ref{fig:family}a: for lollipop-like graphs the cheaper of the two bounds is already accurate to within a typical factor of about 1.3.\n270:\\paragraph{No component attribution.} We evaluated one fixed configuration of the predictor. There are no ablations removing individual features, the interaction term or the ridge penalty, and no sweep over the regularization strength. The size-only reference differs in its whole feature set, so it shows that degree and diameter together add information, not which one matters. The explanations for per-family behaviour in \\S\\ref{sec:discussion} are hypotheses.\n274:\\paragraph{Per-family uncertainty and small families.} Per-family errors of the predictor are reported without intervals. Grids have only five held-out graphs and six training graphs. Per-family paired intervals exist for the gain over the best bound but not for the other comparators. The Matthews, constant and single-bound comparisons are point estimates without paired intervals, and we avoid significance language for them.\n281:We asked whether a handful of cheap global statistics can predict the cover time of small connected graphs more accurately than the classical general-purpose bounds, and how the answer depends on graph type. We evaluated an untuned log-linear ridge regression on the vertex count, edge count, three degree statistics and diameter. On held-out, isomorphism-disjoint graphs from ten families with $n\\le 30$, its typical error is a factor of about 1.20 (mean absolute log error 0.183, CI 0.167--0.207), against about 4.0 for the better of $2m(n-1)$ and $(4/27)n^3$. The paired advantage is significant in nine of ten families. It is largest on complete, Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi and random 3-regular graphs, where the bounds are loosest. On lollipops, where the cubic bound is nearly tight, there is no statistically clear advantage. The predictor itself is least reliable on complete, grid and Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi graphs, and twelve held-out graphs show that statistics-only prediction has an intrinsic ambiguity. Overall, worst-case bounds and fitted predictors answer different questions. The bounds guarantee; the predictor estimates, and it is most useful exactly where a graph is far from the worst case. Testing the stated hypotheses with targeted ablations, extending the size range, and adding richer cheap statistics are the natural next steps.\n312:Random 3-regular & 16 & 0.059 & 2.146 & $-2.088$ & $[-2.303,\\ -1.859]$ \\\\\n330:The evaluator (\\texttt{task/evaluate\\_final.py}) generates deterministic families from a public catalogue (\\texttt{det\\_catalogue}), with pool membership fixed by $n \\bmod 3$. Random families come from \\texttt{random\\_graph}: random trees from uniform Pr\\\"ufer sequences; Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi graphs with $n$ uniform on $\\{5,\\dots,30\\}$, $p$ uniform on $[2\\ln n/n, \\max(0.5, 2\\ln n/n)]$, rejected until connected; random 3-regular graphs via a configuration model with even $n\\in\\{6,\\dots,30\\}$, rejected until simple and connected. Labels come from \\texttt{simulate\\_cover\\_times} with 256 walks per graph. Bootstrap intervals use 1000 replicates of the two-level scheme in \\S\\ref{sec:protocol}. % @evidence[[analysis/roster.json#bootstrap_reps]]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
