{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgTEjmpuFK68VZTtzyWu8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012VA71ZbbxF1yNycAoCUWSP","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; cp paper.tex /tmp/paper.pre_weave.tex; python3 - <<'EOF'\np='paper.tex'; s=open(p).read()\nreps=[\n(\"Feige later showed that the worst case over all $n$-vertex graphs is $(4/27)n^3$ to leading order, attained asymptotically by lollipop graphs, and that every connected graph needs at least about $n\\\\ln n$ steps \\\\citep{Feige1995ATL}.\",\n \"Feige later showed that the worst case over all $n$-vertex graphs is $(4/27)n^3$ to leading order, attained asymptotically by lollipop graphs (for these worst-case results see the survey of \\\\citealp{Lovász2001RandomWO}), and that every connected graph needs at least about $n\\\\ln n$ steps \\\\citep{Feige1995ATL}.\"),\n(\"Feige's upper bound $\\\\tfrac{4}{27}n^3+o(n^3)$ is the exact worst case over $n$-vertex graphs and is approached by lollipops, i.e.\\\\ a clique of about $2n/3$ vertices joined to a path.\",\n \"Feige's upper bound $\\\\tfrac{4}{27}n^3+o(n^3)$ is the exact worst case over $n$-vertex graphs and is approached by lollipops, i.e.\\\\ a clique of about $2n/3$ vertices joined to a path; the survey of \\\\citet{Lovász2001RandomWO} places this result alongside the extremal hitting-time analysis of the lollipop.\"),\n(\"Tighter estimates use more structure. Matthews' method bounds the cover time by the maximum hitting time times $H_{n-1}$.\",\n \"Tighter estimates use more structure. Matthews' method, presented in the survey of \\\\citet{Lovász2001RandomWO}, bounds the cover time by the maximum hitting time times $H_{n-1}$.\"),\n(\"Prior work on cover time is overwhelmingly analytic: it derives bounds that hold for all graphs, or asymptotics for specific families.\",\n \"Prior work on cover time, as collected in the survey of \\\\citet{Lovász2001RandomWO}, is overwhelmingly analytic: it derives bounds that hold for all graphs, and closed forms or growth rates for particular families such as paths, cycles, stars and complete graphs. We do not rely on finer asymptotic results for random families; for random 3-regular, Erd\\\\H{o}s--R\\\\'enyi and grid graphs, all statements in this paper about regimes rest on our own measurements.\"),\n(\"the leading term $(4/27)n^3$ of Feige's upper bound; and\",\n \"the leading term $(4/27)n^3$ of Feige's upper bound (see \\\\citealp{Lovász2001RandomWO}); and\"),\n(\"\\\\item \\\\textbf{Matthews-type reference:} the exact maximum hitting time\",\n \"\\\\item \\\\textbf{Matthews-type reference} (Matthews' bound; see \\\\citealp{Lovász2001RandomWO}): the exact maximum hitting time\"),\n(\"A lollipop with a clique of about $2n/3$ vertices is the shape that asymptotically attains $(4/27)n^3$ \\\\citep{Feige1995ATL}.\",\n \"A lollipop with a clique of about $2n/3$ vertices is the shape that asymptotically attains $(4/27)n^3$ \\\\citep{Lovász2001RandomWO}.\"),\n(\"The latter case is the near-$n\\\\ln n$ regime of dense and expander-like graphs: complete graphs, dense Erd\\\\H{o}s--R\\\\'enyi graphs and random 3-regular graphs.\",\n \"The latter case is exemplified by complete graphs, whose cover time $(n-1)H_{n-1}$ sits at the $n\\\\ln n$ lower-bound regime \\\\citep{Lovász2001RandomWO,Feige1995ATL}. Erd\\\\H{o}s--R\\\\'enyi and random 3-regular graphs show a similarly large bound slack in our measurements (Table~\\\\ref{tab:family_si}); we read this as consistent with a near-$n\\\\ln n$ regime for these families at $n\\\\le 30$, but we did not test that reading separately.\"),\n(\"Gains are smallest on the two families near the bounds' extremal shapes (lollipop and path) and largest on dense and expander-like families, whose cover times are close to the $n\\\\ln n$ lower-bound regime (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:discussion}).\",\n \"Gains are smallest on the two families near the bounds' extremal shapes (lollipop and path). They are largest on complete graphs, which attain the $n\\\\ln n$ lower-bound regime, and on Erd\\\\H{o}s--R\\\\'enyi and random 3-regular graphs, where the measured bound slack is similarly large (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:discussion}).\"),\n]\nfor a,b in reps:\n    assert s.count(a)==1,a[:60]\n    s=s.replace(a,b)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\ngrep -n \"label{tab:\" paper.tex","description":"Apply targeted citation rewrites to paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4471,"cache_read_input_tokens":48099,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4471,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b15765f-ba82-4d49-aaa3-a4d557e16332","uuid":"1ede0bb6-5967-4887-b023-fe7e88ba11bd","timestamp":"2026-10-27T18:45:47.364Z","request_id":"req_011CgTEjm6joZvGt3i3MkKeH"}
